---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Assessment from '../../components/Assessment.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import site from '../../data/site.json';
import { faqSchema } from '../../lib/faq';
const questions = [
  { q: 'Which decisions in this organization are currently being made, or materially influenced, by an algorithmic system?', a: 'Credit scoring, clinical recommendations, hiring decisions and risk classifications are common answers. Most boards cannot name them with specificity.' },
  { q: 'Who is responsible for validating that AI output is accurate before it becomes an action?', a: 'If the answer is "the vendor" or "the technology team", the board does not own the decision. It owns the consequence.' },
  { q: 'When was the last time a human reviewed the model\'s assumptions against current conditions?', a: 'A model optimized for the conditions of three years ago, operating in today\'s market, is active risk.' },
  { q: 'If the model drifted today, producing systematically biased outputs, how long before the board would know?', a: 'The ripple runs the entire time nobody is assigned to watch for it.' },
  { q: 'And who, by name, with a named responsibility, is assigned to watch for that?', a: 'This is not an IT question. It is a governance question, and it lands in the boardroom. The absence of a named person with a named responsibility is the gap. A named owner, a stated goal and reporting the board can see. That is what closes it.' }
];
const page = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebPage',
  '@id': site.url + '/advisory/board-and-leadership', url: site.url + '/advisory/board-and-leadership',
  name: 'Board and Leadership Advisory on AI and Technology Decisions | Jack P. Flaherty',
  description: 'Board and leadership advisory from Jack P. Flaherty. Who owns the technology decision, what it was for, and how the board will know it worked.',
  about: { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Board oversight of AI and technology decisions' },
  author: { '@id': site.url + '/#person' }
};
---
<Base title="Board and Leadership Advisory on AI and Technology Decisions | Jack P. Flaherty"
  description="Board and leadership advisory from Jack P. Flaherty. Who owns the technology decision, what it was for, and how the board will know it worked."
  path="/advisory/board-and-leadership" image="/images/jack-p-flaherty-board-advisory-sepia.jpg" schema={[page, faqSchema(questions)]} hero="/images/jack-p-flaherty-board-advisory-sepia.jpg">

  <div class="board-hero">
    <Hero image="/images/jack-p-flaherty-board-advisory-sepia.jpg" alt="Jack P. Flaherty in conversation with an executive beside an office window" pos="30% 50%" posMobile="30% 40%" short>
      <h1>The board owns the decision. Jack works with the people who answer for it.</h1>
      <p>Board and leadership advisory</p>
      <p>Confidential counsel for boards and executive teams on the technology decision in front of them. Your IT team, your risk function and your counsel each own a piece of it. Jack works on the piece the board owns: who is accountable, what it was for, and how the board will know it was a success. Delivered by Jack, not a team.</p>
      <div class="btns"><a class="btn" href={site.calendly} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a discovery call</a></div>
    </Hero>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="intro">
        <p class="lede">Most boards are approving AI strategies, receiving risk updates and asking thoughtful questions. Then going home believing they have exercised appropriate oversight. The gap between belief and reality is where fiduciary exposure lives.</p>
        <figure class="quote" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
          <blockquote>"Technology changes in ripples, every day, in every tool your people use. The question is whether anyone in your organization is watching, and ready to act on the opportunities those ripples create."</blockquote>
          <figcaption><strong>Jack P. Flaherty</strong></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.6rem">Jack has sat in boardrooms as a Big 4 director, as acting enterprise risk and audit leader for a $10B health system, and as interim COO and risk advisor for fintech and digital-asset companies.</p>
        <p class="lede">The boards that handled new technology well all had one thing in common. They knew, in specific terms, which decisions they were responsible for overseeing.</p>
        <p class="lede">Not which functions reported up through them. Which decisions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="gap-text">
        <h2>The gap</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="color:#E3D5BE">Most boards believe they have governance over new technology. Few can name who owns the decision.</p>
        <p>Boards are approving AI strategies. Receiving risk updates in quarterly packets. Asking thoughtful questions about data governance and model accuracy. Then going home believing they have exercised appropriate oversight.</p>
        <p>The gap is architecture.</p>
        <p>Algorithmic systems are already influencing credit scoring, clinical recommendations, hiring decisions and risk classifications at organizations where the board believes it has control. The systems were deployed at the operational level, optimized for efficiency, and by the time they reached the board packet they looked like a technology update, not a fiduciary exposure.</p>
        <p>The pattern repeats across industries. The sector changes. The failure mode does not.</p>
      </div>
      <h3 class="room">What it looks like in the room</h3>
      <div class="grid c3">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Healthcare</h3>
            <p>A regulator made the cybersecurity risk at a $4B health system unavoidable. Jack built the board proposal, hired and managed the vendors, and the urgent gaps closed on a $2.5 million budget within one year. The organization was fully compliant a year later.</p>
            <p>The board did not freeze. It gave the decision an owner, a budget, a deadline and a review date.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Fintech and digital assets</h3>
            <p>Jack served as interim COO and risk advisor for fintech and digital-asset companies where speed had outpaced every governance structure they had. The founders had vision and funding.</p>
            <p>Nobody had been assigned to watch for the decisions being made without them.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Two settings. Two boards.</h3>
            <p>One failure mode: no named person, with a named responsibility, assigned to watch for the decisions already being made without them.</p>
            <p>That is the problem. That is what Jack fixes.</p>
          </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Assessment />

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Five questions</h2>
      <p class="lede">Five questions worth putting to your audit committee.</p>
      <p>If these questions produce confident, specific, documented answers, the governance is working. If they produce silence, a referral to IT, or a promise to follow up, the ripple is already in the water.</p>
      <div class="grid c2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card accent">
          <h3><span class="num">1.</span>Which decisions in this organization are currently being made, or materially influenced, by an algorithmic system?</h3>
          <p>Credit scoring, clinical recommendations, hiring decisions and risk classifications are common answers. Most boards cannot name them with specificity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent">
          <h3><span class="num">2.</span>Who is responsible for validating that AI output is accurate before it becomes an action?</h3>
          <p>If the answer is "the vendor" or "the technology team", the board does not own the decision. It owns the consequence.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent">
          <h3><span class="num">3.</span>When was the last time a human reviewed the model's assumptions against current conditions?</h3>
          <p>A model optimized for the conditions of three years ago, operating in today's market, is active risk.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent">
          <h3><span class="num">4.</span>If the model drifted today, producing systematically biased outputs, how long before the board would know?</h3>
          <p>The ripple runs the entire time nobody is assigned to watch for it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card accent">
          <h3><span class="num">5.</span>And who, by name, with a named responsibility, is assigned to watch for that?</h3>
          <p>This is not an IT question. It is a governance question, and it lands in the boardroom. The absence of a named person with a named responsibility is the gap. A named owner, a stated goal and reporting the board can see. That is what closes it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What the board receives</h2>
      <p class="lede">Every engagement produces three things the board can hold.</p>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card accent"><h3>A board paper</h3><p>One page. It answers who owns the decision, what it was for and how success will be evidenced.</p></div>
        <div class="card accent"><h3>The questions for management</h3><p>Written for the next quarter's meeting, with the answer each one should produce.</p></div>
        <div class="card accent"><h3>A decision record</h3><p>The owner, the goal, the review date and the reporting the board will see.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem">Advisory is delivered by Jack, not a team. The first 30 days are set on the discovery call.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <h2>Why boards call Jack</h2>
          <p>25 years in risk and advisory, 15 of them at the Big 4, and a method a board can run itself. Built from inside the rooms, not from a governance textbook.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="quote">
          <blockquote>"Most teams leave a strategy session feeling energized. The ones that come back six months later are the ones who left with a shared process, not just a shared poster."</blockquote>
          <figcaption><strong>Jack P. Flaherty</strong></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="grid c4 stats" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>25</strong>Years in risk and advisory, inside the rooms where technology decisions carry the highest cost.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>$10B</strong>Health system where Jack served as acting enterprise risk and audit leader, reporting to the C-suite and audit committee.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>$15B</strong>Financial institution where Jack led a data governance implementation.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><p class="stat"><strong>$2.5M</strong>Board-mandated cybersecurity program for a $4B health system. Urgent gaps closed within one year.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>The next step</h2>
        <p>A 30-minute conversation about which decisions are already being made without your board.</p>
        <div class="btns" style="justify-content:center;margin-top:.4rem"><a class="btn" href={site.calendly} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Book a discovery call</a></div>
        <p class="meta" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Jack replies within one business day and asks questions before he sends a proposal, starting with who is in the room and what decision they are facing. For a department rather than a board, see <a href="/workshops/the-team-writes-the-plan">the workshop</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  .board-hero :global(.hero .wrap) { display: flex; justify-content: flex-end; }
  .board-hero :global(.hero:after) { background: linear-gradient(270deg, rgba(42,26,14,.86) 0%, rgba(42,26,14,.66) 36%, rgba(42,26,14,.1) 64%, rgba(42,26,14,0) 80%); }
  .board-hero :global(.hero .txt) { max-width: 38rem; }
  .board-hero :global(.hero h1) { font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.2vw, 3rem); }
  .intro, .gap-text { max-width: 58rem; }
  .intro p, .gap-text p { max-width: none; }
  .room { margin: 2rem 0 1rem; }
  .quote { margin: 0; }
  .stats .card { padding: 1.3rem; }
  .stat { margin: 0; }
  .stat strong { display: block; font-family: var(--font-head); font-size: 2.4rem; line-height: 1; color: var(--latte); margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .dark .quote blockquote, .dark .quote figcaption, .dark .quote figcaption strong { color: var(--ink); }
</style>
